stream
BT
/F1 11 Tf
15 TL
50 750 Td
() Tj T*
(Part 1: Cellular Respiration Review) Tj T*
(Cellular respiration is the set of metabolic reactions that cells use to convert the) Tj T*
(chemical energy stored in glucose into adenosine triphosphate, commonly called ATP. ATP is) Tj T*
(the main energy currency of the cell and powers processes such as muscle contraction, active) Tj T*
(transport and protein synthesis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Overview of the Process) Tj T*
(Aerobic respiration requires oxygen and occurs in three main stages: glycolysis, the Krebs) Tj T*
(cycle and the electron transport chain. The overall equation shows glucose and oxygen being) Tj T*
(converted into carbon dioxide, water and energy. A single molecule of glucose can yield up) Tj T*
(to 38 molecules of ATP under ideal conditions, although the realistic figure is closer to 30) Tj T*
(to 32 ATP.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Stage 1: Glycolysis) Tj T*
(Glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm and does not require oxygen. During glycolysis one) Tj T*
(molecule of glucose is split into two molecules of pyruvate. This stage produces a net gain) Tj T*
(of two ATP molecules and two molecules of NADH, which carry high energy electrons to later) Tj T*
(stages.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Stage 2: The Krebs Cycle) Tj T*
(The Krebs cycle, also known as the citric acid cycle, takes place in the mitochondrial) Tj T*
(matrix. Before the cycle begins, pyruvate is converted into acetyl coenzyme A, releasing) Tj T*
(carbon dioxide. Each turn of the Krebs cycle produces one ATP, three NADH and one FADH2, and) Tj T*
(releases two molecules of carbon dioxide as waste.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Stage 3: Electron Transport Chain) Tj T*
(The electron transport chain is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. NADH and FADH2) Tj T*
(donate electrons to a series of protein complexes, and the energy released pumps hydrogen) Tj T*
(ions across the membrane. The resulting gradient drives the enzyme ATP synthase, a process) Tj T*
(called oxidative phosphorylation, which produces the majority of ATP. Oxygen acts as the) Tj T*
(final electron acceptor and combines with hydrogen ions to form water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Anaerobic Respiration) Tj T*
(When oxygen is unavailable, cells rely on fermentation to regenerate NAD+ so that glycolysis) Tj T*
(can continue. In human muscle cells, pyruvate is converted into lactic acid, which) Tj T*
(contributes to muscle fatigue during intense exercise. In yeast, alcoholic fermentation) Tj T*
(converts pyruvate into ethanol and carbon dioxide. Fermentation produces only two ATP per) Tj T*
(glucose molecule, making it far less efficient than aerobic respiration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Key Exam Points) Tj T*
(Students should be able to name the location of each stage, state the inputs and outputs of) Tj T*
(glycolysis, explain why oxygen is essential as the final electron acceptor, and compare the) Tj T*
(ATP yield of aerobic respiration with fermentation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Part 2: Cellular Respiration Review) Tj T*
ET
endstream
